==German==
{{wikipedia|lang=de}}

===Etymology===
From or related to {{der|de|ML.|Oddara}}, said by Udolph to be from {{der|de|xil|*Adra|t=water vein}}, related to {{cog|ae|𐬀𐬛𐬎|t=watercourse}}, {{cog|txh|urda|t=stream}}, which could ultimately be from {{der|de|ine-pro|*h₁eh₁t-|t=to breathe}}, which would make it a doublet of {{m|de|Ader|t=vein, blood vessel}}.<ref>Monumenta Germaniae Historica: Res gesta Hammaburgensis ecclesiae, S. 76</ref><ref> Kluge: Etymologisches Wörterbuch der deutschen Sprache, 24. Auflage, 2002</ref><ref> Langenscheidts Taschenwörterbuch Altgriechisch, 8. Auflage, 2000 </ref> Folk etymology connected it to {{cog|pl|drzeć|t=to rend, penetrate, tear}} as in "break through to the sea."

